http:\/\/online.wsj.com\/article\/SB10001424052748704206804575467440809287142.html?mod=WSJ_mgmt_MiddleSecondHighlights
To the Top
To compile the 2010 Wall Street Journal report and rankings of executive M.
B.
A.
programs, we again worked with Management Research Group and Critical Insights, both of Portland, Maine, to survey thousands of students and hundreds of companies about the quality of E.
M.
B.
A.
programs and how well they deliver on improving management and leadership skills.
Our top 25 is based on responses from 3,060 of the most recent graduates of 87 E.
M.
B.
A.
programs at 64 business schools with campuses in 18 countries; responses from 189 corporate human-resources and executive-development managers; and a measurement of how well students say their schools enhanced the skills that companies said were most important.
Working with Management Research Group, we calculated the total score for each school—and thus the ranking—by combining the alumni score (40% of the total), corporate score (40% of the total) and skills score (20% of the total).
(For more on how the rankings were arrived at, you can see a full methodology .)
Based purely on ratings for skills, the top program belonged to Thunderbird School of Global Management.
Graduates gave it high marks for instruction in strategic thinking, general leadership and the ability to work across multiple functional areas.
Northwestern's Kellogg, meanwhile, dropped four slots from 2008 in the overall ranking, despite the fact that in our corporate ranking it far outpaced all other programs.
Kellogg graduates stand apart for their ability to \"step forward in a calm and direct manner\" under stressful conditions, says Bill Corley, director of human resources at Northstar Aerospace Inc.
But student satisfaction varied across the school's seven programs (some of which are delivered with partner schools).
That pulled Kellogg's overall student satisfaction and skills scores down considerably."],[[],["En el [f-blue]primer [\/f-blue]puesto [f-red]superior[\/f-red] [[f-blue]\"Top\" is, usually, translated as \"superior\" but in this case the article is talking about the position in a ranking. I don't recall any specific term which we use to refer to the \"top\" in this context other than first place.][\/f-blue]"],["[f-blue]Para [\/f-blue][sline][f-red]Compliando[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-blue]compilar [is correct but sounds weird. I would use \"preparar\" instead.] [\/f-blue]el informe del Wall Street Journal [sline][f-red]en[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-red]de [\/f-red]2010 y la clasificación del programa de EMBA, trabaja[f-red][sline]ba[\/sline][\/f-red]mos de nuevo con el gr[sline][f-red]o[\/f-red][\/sline]upo de investigación de administra[f-red]ción[\/f-red] y [f-blue]con [\/f-blue]Critical [f-red]I[\/f-red]nsights (¿aquí [sline][f-red]lo[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-blue]el [\/f-blue]nombre [sline][f-red]de[\/f-red][\/sline] originlal en inglés es mejor? [f-blue][Definitively. As a rule of thumb, company names shouldn't be translated, much like people's names.][\/f-blue]), ambos de Portland, Maine, [sline][f-red]a[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-blue]para [\/f-blue]estudiar miles de [sline]los[\/sline] estudiantes y cientos de [sline]las[\/sline] compañias sobre la calidad del programa de EMBA y [f-blue]de [\/f-blue]c[f-red]ó[\/f-red]m[f-red]o[\/f-red] [sline]entrenan y[\/sline] [f-blue]propocionan una [\/f-blue]mejora[f-red]n[\/f-red] [f-blue]en [\/f-blue]las hab[f-red]i[\/f-red]li[f-red][sline]a[\/sline][\/f-red]dades de administra[f-red]ción[\/f-red] y liderazgo. [f-blue][management can be both \"administrador(es)\" and \"administración\". \"Administrador\" is the person which is in charge of management.][\/f-blue]"],["[f-red]Nosotros[\/f-red] [f-blue]Nuestros [\/f-blue][sline]los[\/sline] primeros 25 puestos [f-blue][that is a correct translation, but nowadays people usually use the English term. Thus most people would say \"Nuestro top 25\"][\/f-blue] est[f-red]á[\/f-red]n basa[f-red]dos [\/f-red][f-blue]en[\/f-blue] las respuestas de [sline]los[\/sline] 3060 [f-blue]estudiantes [\/f-blue]graduado[sline][f-red]re[\/f-red][\/sline]s reci[f-red]e[\/f-red]n[f-blue]temente[\/f-blue] de [sline]los[\/sline] ochenta y siete programa de EMBA [f-blue]en 64 escuelas de empresariales con [\/f-blue][sline]que hay[\/sline] campus[sline][f-red]es[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-blue][campus is the same in singular and plural, even in English!] [\/f-blue]en 18 pa[f-red]í[\/f-red]ses, [sline]se responsan desde[\/sline] [f-blue]de [\/f-blue]los encargandos de recursos hum[f-red]a[\/f-red]nos y desarrollo ejecutivo (??) de 189 empresas."],["y [f-blue]de [\/f-blue]la evaluaci[f-red]ó[\/f-red]n de [sline][f-red]como[\/f-red][\/sline] los estudiante [f-blue]de cómo [\/f-blue][sline]dicen[\/sline] sus clases [sline][f-red]para mejorar[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-blue]mejoraron [\/f-blue]sus hablidades [sline][f-red]y[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-blue]que [\/f-blue]las empresas [sline][f-red]dichoes[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-blue][f-bold]dijeron que [\/f-bold][\/f-blue][sline][f-red][f-bold]fueron[\/f-bold][\/f-red][\/sline][f-bold] [f-blue]eran [\/f-blue][sline][f-red]el[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-blue]las [\/f-blue]m[f-red]á[\/f-red]s importante[f-blue]s[\/f-blue][\/f-bold]. [f-blue][or simply \"que las empresas consideran más importantes\". The translation is not so literal, but the meaning is the same and sounds more natural in Spanish.][\/f-blue]"],["Tra[sline][f-red]n[\/f-red][\/sline]bajando junto con Management Research Group, calculamos las puntuación[sline][f-red]es[\/f-red][\/sline] total[sline][f-red]idad[\/f-red][\/sline] de cada escuela, y de este modo la clasificación, [sline][f-red]por aumentar[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-blue]combinando [\/f-blue]las puntuaci[f-red]o[\/f-red]nes de los estudiante[f-blue]s [\/f-blue](cuarenta por ciento [sline][f-red]de la[\/f-red][\/sline] [f-blue]del [\/f-blue]todal[