texto original
http:\/\/online.wsj.com\/article\/SB10001424052748704206804575467440809287142.html?mod=WSJ_mgmt_MiddleSecondHighlights
Mr.
Hums performs a return-on-investment analysis of each student's experience.
A good return, he says, means that in addition to the employees' personal goals being met, their managers notice an improvement in performance, and the employees attribute their growth to the program.
Overall, despite some reservations, Mr.
Hums says he sees the value in exposing his company's E.
M.
B.
A.
students to the outside business world.
Some schools, aware of such skepticism, try to include course work that can be of direct benefit to students' employers.
At Kellogg, students are encouraged to work on projects in class directly related to their company.
At Wharton, E.
M.
B.
A.
s can work one-on-one with faculty to design an independent-study thesis, often directly applicable to their jobs.
Students in top-ranked programs, meanwhile, insist that they see a difference in their performance—regardless of what their companies might say.
\"I don't think the firm sees a return in the way I see a return,\" says Mr.
Yoshizaki, a May graduate of Wharton's Philadelphia program who is now a director at PricewaterhouseCoopers.
\"But I see a difference in the way I am working.\"
— Diana Middleton and Joe Walker contributed to this article.
Ms.
Porter is a writer in New York.
Email her at reports@wsj.
