[sline]Yo[\/sline] [f-blue](you can just leave it, because the conjugation of the verb already implies it's you the one who does the action)[\/f-blue] voy a [f-blue]ir a[\/f-blue] [f-blue](you're talking bout the future)[\/f-blue] clase a est[f-red]udiar [\/f-red]español mañana por la mañana, después voy a [f-blue]ir a [\/f-blue]casa de mi abuela. [f-blue]It's better this way: [\/f-blue]Mañana por la mañana voy a ir a clase a estudiar español, después iré a casa de mi abuela. [f-blue](When you talk about \"when you do something\", you can put it in many places of the sentence, but it's better if you put it at the beginning. When you talk about something you're gonna do tomorrow, you use future \"voy a ir\", and as you're telling something you're gonna do afterwards, even more in the future, you use the form \"iré\", another conjugation for the future)[\/f-blue]"],["La clase emp[f-red]i[\/f-red]eza a las nueve y media y termina a la un[f-red]a[\/f-red] y media.
