"You've Got It All Wrong
A few weeks ago, I met someone who happened to be in the same faculty with me.
She is a student in the forth year, but she failed in a course when she was in the same year as me.
So, she is supposed to pass this course this year or she is not going to graduate.
She seemed like a nice girl.
We exchanged our phone numbers and made promises of keeping in touch.
A few days later, she called me.
She said that she wanted to be with me in the group that we were going to form for the final project in this course and that she doesn't now anyone except me in this course.
I told her that I'm not sure what my friends will say about it, so I have to take their permission first before counting her in.
She was like, okay.
But I knew that she already counted herself in.
I had the feeling that she wasn't going to be the kind of hard working student and that she was going to count on us to do all the work for her.
Later, I discovered that my intuition wasn't a false alarm.
She did want to count on us to do everything for her under the label of mercy.
After a few days, I had a long conversation with her over the phone.
She told me the story of some girl who was in the forth year when she was in the third.
The girl also failed in a course from her third year.
This friend of mine helped her with this course, well, I don't know if it's fair to call it help.
She basically did all the assignments required for her.
After telling me this story, I had a feeling that she was implying that I should do the same with her.
I listened to the whole story, then it was my turn to talk.
I told her that I don't think what she did was right.
Personally, I'd prefer to prepare myself to receiving bad grades than prepare myself to receiving a grade that I don't deserve.
She kept saying that I should be more considerate and  help people.
I told her that I could stay up all night trying to help someone to understand something, but not to help them cheat.
I had been offered many times to have my assignments done for me when I couldn't do them and I strongly refused.
How can I get grades for something I have never knew or understand?
This just seemed illogical.
The more I talked the more confused she became.
I think it's sad that what is wrong is considered normal, now, and who does the right thing, is considered not cooperative or old-fashioned.
The world has gone upside down.
Many people have got it totally wrong.
So, what do think of my reaction?
Was it wrong?"],[[],["A few weeks ago, I met someone who [f-blue]happens[\/f-blue] to be in the same faculty with me."],["She is a student in the forth year, but she failed[sline] in[\/sline] a course when she was in the same year as me.
She is in [f-blue]her fourth year[\/f-blue], but she failed [sline]in[\/sline] a course when she was in the same year as me."],["So she [f-blue]has to pass this course or she won't graduate.[\/f-blue]"],[],["We exchanged [sline]our[\/sline] phone numbers and [f-blue]promised to keep[\/f-blue] in touch."],[],["She said that she wanted to be [f-blue]in a group we formed to work on the final project, and that she doesn't know anyone in the course except me.[\/f-blue]"],["I told her that I'm not sure what my friends will say about it, so I have to [f-blue]get[\/f-blue] their permission [sline]first[\/sline] before counting her in."],["She was [sline]like, [\/sline]okay [f-red]with that[\/f-red]. (or she seemed to be okay with that.)[f-blue]'like' is a slang used as a filler word in speech. Try not to use it in formal writing.[\/f-blue] "],[],["I had the feeling that she wasn't going to be [f-blue]a[\/f-blue] [sline]the kind of[\/sline] hardworking student and that she was going to count on us to do all the work for her."],["Later, I discovered that my intuition was [f-blue]right.[\/f-blue]"],["She did want us [f-blue]to do all the work out of pity.[\/f-blue]"],[],[],["[f-blue]This[\/f-blue] girl also failed in a course [f-blue]in[\/f-blue] her third year."],["This friend of mine helped her with this course. Well, I don't know if it's fair to call it help."],["She basically did all the assignments [f-blue]for her.[\/f-blue]"],["After telling me this story, I had a feeling that she was implying that I should do the same [sline]with her[\/sline]."],[],[],["Personally, I'd[f-blue] rather get bad grades than good grades I don't deserve.[\/f-blue]"],[],[],[],["How can I get grades for something I [sline]have never knew or understand? [f-red][\/f-red][\/sline][f-red]don't know or understand?[\/f-red][sline][f-red][\/f-red][\/sline]"],[],[],["I think it's sad that [f-blue]this is considered normal now and those who do the right thing are considered non-cooperative or old-fashioned.[\/f-blue]"],[],[],[],[]
"Quiero empezar a escribir buenas entradas
No quiero seguir escribiendo entradas trivial.
Así que voy a tratar de terminar todo el curso de español en un día.
Luego,  voy a tratar de escribir más entradas.
Ellos no van a ser buenos al principio, pero voy a seguir tratando.
Gracias."],[[],["No quiero seguir escribiendo entradas [f-blue]triviales[\/f-blue]."],[],[],["Ell[f-red]a[\/f-red]s no [f-blue]van a ser\/serán[\/f-blue] buen[f-red]a[\/f-red]s al principio, pero voy a seguir [f-red]intentándolo[\/f-red]. [f-blue](\"Entradas\" es femenino, por lo cual tienes que referirte como \"Ellas\" y \"buenas\")[\/f-blue]"],[]
"Episodio 1 - Cailluo - El desayuno sorpresa de cailluo
[Podrían corregir mi español.
