"Unas preguntas en español
1) ¿Cuales son el leve malestar y la sensación de fuertemente querer vomitar?
n_____
2) ¿Cuál es la persona que colecta cada día importantes noticias para reportar en un periódico o una televisión?
También hace entrevistas con los famosos personajes.
3) ¿Cuál es la partícula básica que representa la unidad más pequeña de un elemento químico?
4) En el sistema judicial de los Estados Unidos, es un grupo selectado de ciudadanos ordinarios que decide si el acusado de un delito es culpable o no.
Si se decide que el acusado es culpable, entonces dicta el juez la sentencia.
¿Cuál es este grupo de ciudadanos?"],[[],[],[],[],[],["3) ¿Cuál es la partícula básica que representa la unidad más pequeña de un elemento químico? Átomo"],[],[],["¿Cuál es este grupo de ciudadanos? Jurado"]
"Oversimplification and languages
or “How does a child learn a language?\" (a hypothesis)
Einstein once said “everything should be made as simple as possible, but not simpler”.
If something is made simpler than possible, of course it becomes incorrect.
This remark of his was probably meant as a warning to some scientists against their tendency or temptation to oversimplify complex things when they get too complicated.
In science that is a completely reasonable and desirable attitude.
However, it is a well-known fact that a baby or a little child does oversimplify the grammar of a language to learn it so well.
Luckily, the child is not (yet) a scientist.
Every child makes errors of the following type, for example: “I eated it.” Of course that’s incorrect.
It should have said “I ate it.” What’s surprising here, though, is that the child regularized a grammar rule which it had never learned.
It knows by observation that the past form of a verb usually has –ed for an ending, and it applied that oversimplified rule to the verb “eat”.
The result is wrong, but fortunately it doesn’t matter too much for obvious reasons (one of them being that its parents are usually very supportive and are not critical at all about such errors) and also because it doesn’t have to take a test yet.
Besides the error can be easily fixed sooner or later after getting some feedback from others.
One might say that learning a language is not an exact science, but a kind of fuzzy, zigzag (or iterative) process.
However, the end product may become nearly perfect.
(Let’s assume that an ordinary person speaks her\/his native language nearly perfectly.) We should remember the proverb “All’s well that ends well”.
A painter doesn’t try to make his painting perfect at every stage of its making, but he just wants to make the finished work look great.
(By the way this applies to science as well.
The discovery process is highly irrational and zigzag also, and they just need to make the finished work good.)
I tend to believe that a big reason that many people find it hard to learn how to speak a foreign language may be that they don’t want to say “I eated it” even once, because they don’t want to lose face in public.
This may be largely the product of the culture which overemphasizes performance and accuracy.
Cast in this light this kind of attitude may clearly sound absurd, but it may not be obvious to the person in a given circumstance.
That’s perhaps why it is a good idea to change your point of view from time to time and try to look at things from a different angle.
(subject to revision)
Any comments, corrections and discussions are welcome.
Thanks!"],[[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[]
