"A game for Spanish
This is a Spanish version of a game I proposed recently for English and some other languages.
(See the entry entitled \"A game for English\" for more details.) 
The rule: 
the last letter of the word being explained is used as the first letter of the next word.
If you find the answer, write it down below the explanation.
Then pick a word beginning with the last letter of that word and then EXPLAIN it in Spanish instead of directly saying what the word is.
Thanks in advance for your active participation!
If the word ends with a letter which cannot be used in the beginning of a word, just use the letter right before it as the first letter of your next word.
Here is an explanation for the first word:
1) ¿Cual es el juguete en un parco que consta de una larga plancha horizontal con dos o más sillas?
Unos niños pueden jugar balanceándose sobre él.
b_____"],[[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[]
"Why all these questions? #1
Why am I making up all these simple (or silly) questions?
1) First of all, because it is not easy for me to find something interesting to write about (or say) everyday, especially in a foreign language which I am trying to learn.
2) I hate to learn words and rules of grammar by rote memorization.
I want to learn them (much more easily) by actually using them for an interesting activity.
3) I want to get a lot of active practice.
So I need to find a very routine and easy activity which I can use whenever I have some free time.
4) The solution to 1-3 is just to practice explaining common things or words: in writing, in thinking, and in speaking; with or (later) without a dictionary.
5) You can always find lots of interesting things to explain or describe.
Anything around you or any (reasonable) word in a dictionary, they may all be good choices.
6) While you struggle to explain something, you naturally find the basic words which you need most.
7) Also, the words you actually use for a practical purpose (namely, to explain something) tend to remain in your memory.
After using a word a few times, you will become familiar with it.
Then you can easily recall it later when you need it.
8) How can a child learn a language so quickly and efficiently?
I think that's because it is so eager to express itself and also it is fearless.
So it does not care so much about making errors.
Just look around you if you are not sure.
Perhaps you will see that anyone who acts like a child in that respect (i.
e.
eager and fearless) can learn almost as well as a child.
What do you think?
(to be continued)"],[[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[]
"Preguntas, adivinanzas o “dicciocicios” 10
1.
En la historia de los Estados Unidos hubo un período que parece un poco extraño ahora.
Se trata del período alrededor de los años 1920-1930, durante el que la fabricación y venta de las bebidas alcohólicas eran ilegales.
Ese acontecimiento no era bueno para la moralidad de la gente, porque mucha gente bebía secretamente, incluso los juices, policieros y aun el presidente del país.
También era culpable para el desarrollo de los crímenes organizados.
¿Cómo se llama este período?
P_________ \/ L___ s____
2.
Es una lata de metal duro.
Es cilíndrico y tiene una largueza de alrededor de un pie.
Siempre se pinta roja.
Sirve para apagar el fuego, cuando sucede un incendio.
Contiene un líquido no inflamable.
Tiene una manga de metal y también una manguera de goma.
Para apagar el fuego, hay que tener apretada la manga, mantiendo la boca de la manguera hacia el fuego.
Entonces, se echa un chorro fuerte de espuma y cubre el fuego.
Eso puede apagarlo, si tienes un poco de suerte.
Si no funciona bien, tienes que huir rápido.
