"A letter to my old landlady!
There's some annoying stuff going on with my old landlady and I need to write her a letter, any help (on the Spanish part) would be greatly appreciated!
:)
(First in English, just skip this!)
Good afternoon Leiticia,
I would like to speak with you about what is going on with the apartment.
It is turning into a big deal and I'd like things to stay nice between us.
Paolo was preparing a new burofax announcing legal actions.
I told him to wait (I think he's being a bit too aggressive) and that I would have talked to you.
I'm sure we can settle this situation in a friendly way.
Maybe our Burofax was not interpreted correctly.
We're mostly in disagreement about having to pay for the the mattress (NOTE: in Spanish here they call it colchon) because:
1) The new mattress you have bought, according to the bill, is a latex one.
Not the same kind as the original and certainly more expensive.
2) A broken spring in the mattresses is still to be attributed to the normal decay and usage and that is not our responsibility.
I hope to hear from you soon and discuss.
<insert something formally nice here>
Regards,
-
Buenas tardes Leticia,
Quisiera hablar contigo sobre que está pasando con el piso.
Está convertiendo en una gran cosa y quiero que las cosas entre nosotros se quedan bien.
Paolo ha preparado un burofax nuevo annunciando los acciones legals.
He dicho a el de attender (creo que el está un poco agresivo) y que voy a hablar contigo.
Estoy seguro que podemos soluncionar esto en una manera amistosa.
A lo mejor nuesto Burofax no era interpretado bien.
Estamos mayormente en desacuerdo que debemos pagar para el colchón, por qué:
1) El colchón nuevo que has comprado, según la factura, es de látex.
Eso no es lo mismo que el colchón de anterior y es por cierto más caro.
2) Un muelle roto en un colchón es también parte del desgaste natural y eso no es la responsabilidad nuestra.
Espero tu respuesta y de discutir.
Atentamente,
Jessica 
-
