[f-blue]Hay un ventilador[\/f-blue][f-red][f-bold] detrás[\/f-bold][\/f-red][f-blue] de la mujer.[\/f-blue]"],[],["12- Hay una maleta [sline]alrededor de ([\/sline]cerca de[sline])[\/sline] el pupitre.
 [f-blue]Hay una maleta [\/f-blue][f-bold][f-red]cerca de[\/f-red][\/f-bold][f-red][f-bold]l[\/f-bold] [\/f-red][f-blue]pupitre. (This one is more natural. If you say *[\/f-blue]Hay una maleta alrededor del pupitre[f-blue]* it means that the desk is surrounded or enclosed by the suitcase. To say that something is somewhere around or somewhere near something else you say *cerca de(l \/ la) *. [\/f-blue])"],[],[],["¿ [f-bold]Cuál [\/f-bold]es la diferencia entre [te amo] y [te quiero]?
[f-blue]¿[\/f-blue][f-bold][f-red]Cuál[\/f-red][\/f-bold][f-blue] es la diferencia entre [te amo] y [te quiero]? [\/f-blue][f-blue]*Te amo* literally means *I love you* It is OK to say it to your sisters for example, or your family, but I do not recommend you to say it to your friends, because that would mean that you *love her* and you want her to be your girlfriend, and you don´t even say it to your girlfriend until the relationship is stable and mature. I have friends that were dumped because they said *Te amo* after 2 day of dating their girlfriends and they simply said *I am sorry but I am not ready to say that I love you*. I say *Te amo* to my friends, but just when joking or when they are EXTREMELY close friends (like friends I have known my whole life). As for *Te quiero* literally means *I like you* BUT It has a stronger feeling. That is what you say to girlfriend when the situation is not appropriate to say *Te amo* yet. You can also say it to your family, it is more common than *Te amo* in that way. But if you are male and you say it to your male friends they will think you are gay, so be careful. If you want to say you like someone its more common to say *Me agradas*. This one you can say it to anybody you know or even someone you just met without leading to misunderstandings. [\/f-blue]"],[],[],[],[]
