"My second day of learning Spanish
¡Hola, amigos!
Last time you really helped me!
Thank you!
Today I have new questions, would you be so kind to help me out?
Question 1:
When do you pronounce fricative (fricativo) b [β]?
I understand how to pronounce it, but I have 4 text-books on Spanish and they have different explanations on when to pronounce it occlusively (oclusivo)!
For example:
Book A: Pronounce occlusive (oclusivo) sound [β] in the beginning of a word, after a pause, after sound [m].
Book B: Pronounce occlusive (oclusivo) sound [β] in the beginning of a word after a pause and also after letters m and n.
Book C: In the beginning of a word and after m and n.
Book C looks the most incorrect to me, because we know that sometimes b in the beginning of a word is pronounced fricatively.
But what does \"after a pause\" mean?
For example, is there a pause between \"la\" and \"vida\" in \"la vida\"?
Is there a pause in \"de boca\"?
Can you please give me examples when b in the beginning of word is pronounced occlusively and when it is pronounced fricatively (also in the beginning of a word)?
I know it's a long question.
But it's really hard question for those who learn Spanish and it seems like most text-books don't explain it very well.
So, please help...
Question 2:
Almost the same problem is with letter d.
Text-books say that it is pronounced occusively in the beginning of a word, after a pause, and also after l and n.
Again, I don't understand what they mean saying \"after a pause\".
Are there cases when d is pronounced fricatively in the beginning of a word?
Question 3:
In the word \"Adivina\" - am I right when I think that d and v in this word are pronounced the same way?
Phonetic rules say they have to be pronounced different ways, but I heard this word pronounced by a Spanish person and letters d and v in that word sounded absolutely the same to me.
Question 4:
Can you please check my pronunciation of the fricative d?
I recorded myself:
http:\/\/bazanov.net\/spanish2.wma
Question 5:
Again, different text-books contain different rules on pronunciation of the letter x.
Some books say that begore consonants (consonante) it is pronounced as [s], but in the Spanish speech I think I always hear it as [ks] before consonants, for example texto - I hear [teksto], excusa - I hear [ekskusa].
Also, am I right that in the beginning of a word it is always pronounced as [s]?
Question 6:
How do you translate \"saludos\" in the end of a text\/speech?
Do you translate it \"good bye\", or maybe \"with best regards\", or how?
Question 7:
Sometimes I see someone answers \"Un poco.\".
Why is there the article \"un\" before the adverb \"poco\"?
For example, in English language there are almost always no articles before adverbs.
I know that's a lot of questions, but the answers will be useful for money people in the future (for example my friend also learns Spanish and will read our discussion)!
¡Muchas gracias de antemano!"],[[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[]
"My first day of learning Spanish
Hello!
I am from Russia and I also speak English.
Yesterday I decided to learn Spanish!
At the moment I understand the basics of grammar but I have questions on pronunciation:
1) Pronunciation of the letters b and v.
Can you please explain when I have to pronounce them as [b] and when I have to pronounce them another way.
Also I don't completely understand that other way of pronouncing them.
Is it close to [v]?
Or is it like I want to say [b] but don't finish it?
2) Pronunciation of the letter d.
As I understand in the beginning of a sentence, after a pause and after l and n it is pronounced as hard [d].
In other cases it is pronounced as soft [d] - but how to pronounce it?
Is it similar to the English [w]?
And do you pronounce it in the end of a word, for example in usted?
3) Pronunciation of the \"ll\".
In most of my exercise books it is written that this sound is pronounced as a very soft [l], but when I listen to a Spanish speech, I hear it as [j] (like the last sound in \"hey\"), or as soft [d].
For example - Ellos - sometimes I hear it as [Eyos] or as [Edios] (with very soft d).
4) Pronunciation of c = as [k] or as [f], right?
For example - cocina = [kofina]?
5) Pronunciation of z = something average between [f] and [s], right?
Thank you very much for the help!"],[[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[]
